[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted — tab session, [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.

  Next question, and it's the one that decides whether the store is a safe convenience or a security boundary:

  [1mThe fork:[0m is the stored identity a [3mlabel[0m or a [3mcredential[0m? Both look identical in the code — a userId in storage — but
  they have opposite threat models, and retrofitting the distinction later means auditing every call site.

  - [1mLabel only[0m — the userId is metadata: it goes into log lines and rides along on form submissions so you can see who
    did what. Every request is independently authenticated by the server through its own mechanism. If someone edits the
    stored value, they corrupt their own logs and nothing else. This is the version where [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is
    unambiguously fine.
  - [1mCarries an auth token too[0m — the store also holds something the app sends to prove identity. Now the store is worth
    attacking, and [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is the wrong home for it: anything running on the page can read it. The standard
    answer is an httpOnly cookie the server sets, which JavaScript can't touch — which in turn means the identity store
    and the credential live in two different places by design.
  - [1mUsed for client-side gating[0m — the app reads the stored userId to decide what to show or permit. This is the hole I
    flagged: a user edits one value and becomes someone else. I'm listing it because apps drift into it accidentally,
    not because I'd recommend it.

  My recommendation is label only, with the server authenticating independently. It's the only option where the thing
  we're building stays small, and it doesn't rule anything out — adding an httpOnly session cookie later is server-side
  work that doesn't disturb the client store at all.
